<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Barcelona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Barcino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Barcelona is the capital of Catalonia and the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llobregat">Llobregat</a> and Besòs rivers. Founded as the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcino">Barcino</a>, it was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 801 and became the seat of the counts of Barcelona, whose union with Aragon in 1137 created the Crown of Aragon. It later grew into a leading port and industrial city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcelona.jpg" alt="Barcelona" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M McBey · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_light_over_Barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ruled for Hisham II from the late 970s, reorganised the army around Berber and Saqaliba regiments and led more than fifty campaigns against the Christian kingdoms, sacking <b>Barcelona</b> in 985, León in 988 and Santiago de Compostela in 997, from which he carried the cathedral’s bells to Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…a Frankish noble, Chorson, as count of Toulouse; after Chorson was captured by Basques and deposed in 790, the county passed to William of Gellone, a kinsman of the king, who defended the Spanish March against Umayyad raids and took part in the capture of <b>Barcelona</b> in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their son, born Ramon and reigning as Alfonso II, succeeded to <b>Barcelona</b> in 1162 and to Aragon on his mother’s abdication in 1164.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirs sent regular summer campaigns against the north: under Hisham I armies sacked Oviedo in 794 and 795 and raided Septimania in 793, though the Franks took <b>Barcelona</b> in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mediterranean coast and the Ebro valley had felt Greek, Phoenician and Iberian influence for centuries and became thoroughly Roman, with cities such as Tarraco, Barcino (<b>Barcelona</b>), Emporiae, Valentia, Saguntum, Carthago Nova and Caesaraugusta (Zaragoza).</blockquote><a href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Hispania Tarraconensis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Alfonso I's death, his brother Ramiro II, a monk recalled from the cloister to rule, fathered a daughter, Petronila, and in 1137 betrothed her to Ramon Berenguer IV, count of <b>Barcelona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
